# Dugan - Nursing Assisting: A Foundation in Caregiving.
# Chapter list: 6th edition, from Hartman Publishing's sample table of contents (hartmanonline.com).
# Summaries cover each chapter's standard topics; they are written for this site, not copied from the book.

C = [
    (1, "The Nursing Assistant in Long-Term Care", [
        "Nursing assistants (NAs) work in long-term care, hospitals, home health, assisted living, and other settings, providing basic personal care under the supervision of licensed nurses. Long-term care residents often have chronic conditions and need help with activities of daily living (ADLs) such as bathing, dressing, eating, and toileting. Medicare and Medicaid pay for much long-term care.",
        "The care team includes the resident (the most important member), nurses, physicians, therapists, social workers, and dietitians. NAs follow the chain of command and the care plan, and nurses delegate tasks using the Five Rights of Delegation: right task, circumstances, person, directions and communication, and supervision. Professionalism includes grooming, dependability, and staying within scope of practice.",
    ], ["Resident is the most important team member", "ADLs: bathing, dressing, eating, toileting", "Five Rights of Delegation", "Follow the chain of command"]),
    (2, "Ethical and Legal Issues", [
        "Law is rules set by government; ethics is knowledge of right and wrong. The Omnibus Budget Reconciliation Act (OBRA, 1987) set standards for NA training (minimum 75 hours), competency evaluation, state registries, and Residents' Rights.",
        "Residents' Rights include quality care, privacy, dignity, making choices, voicing complaints without fear, managing their own money, and freedom from abuse and restraints. Types of abuse include physical, psychological, verbal, sexual, financial abuse, and neglect; NAs must report suspected abuse. HIPAA protects health information, and the ombudsman advocates for residents.",
    ], ["OBRA: training, registries, Residents' Rights", "Report suspected abuse", "Ombudsman advocates for residents", "HIPAA protects privacy"]),
    (3, "Communication Skills", [
        "Communication is verbal (spoken or written) and nonverbal (body language, facial expressions). Barriers include medical jargon, language differences, and clichés. Good communicators use active listening and avoid asking 'why' questions or giving advice.",
        "NAs document observations objectively. Objective (signs) are things you can see, hear, touch, or smell; subjective (symptoms) are what the resident reports. NAs use basic medical terminology and abbreviations, convert to military (24-hour) time, report changes promptly, use the call system, and fill out incident reports.",
    ], ["Objective = observed; subjective = reported", "Military time: 3 PM = 1500", "Document facts, not opinions", "Complete incident reports"]),
    (4, "Communication Challenges", [
        "Some residents have conditions that make communication harder. For hearing impairment: face the resident, speak clearly, reduce noise, and check hearing aids. For vision impairment: identify yourself, explain what you're doing, and keep items in the same place.",
        "Other challenges include speech problems after stroke (aphasia), confusion and dementia, anger or combative behavior, and depression. NAs stay calm, use simple words, and never argue.",
    ], ["Face residents with hearing loss", "Identify yourself to residents with vision loss", "Aphasia = trouble with speech", "Stay calm with combative residents"]),
    (5, "Diversity and Human Needs and Development", [
        "Maslow's hierarchy of needs: physical needs come first, then safety, love and belonging, self-esteem, and self-actualization. Holistic care treats the whole person: body, mind, and spirit. NAs respect cultural and religious differences and residents' sexuality.",
        "Human development moves through life stages from infancy to older adulthood. Normal aging includes thinner skin, weaker muscles, and slower reactions, but confusion is not normal. Developmental and intellectual disabilities and mental health conditions also affect care needs.",
    ], ["Maslow: physical needs first", "Holistic = whole person", "Respect cultural differences", "Confusion is not normal aging"]),
    (6, "Infection Prevention and Control", [
        "The chain of infection has six links: causative agent, reservoir, portal of exit, mode of transmission, portal of entry, and susceptible host. Medical asepsis reduces pathogens; hand hygiene is the most important way to prevent infection.",
        "Standard Precautions treat all blood, body fluids, nonintact skin, and mucous membranes as potentially infectious. Transmission-Based Precautions add airborne, droplet, or contact measures. PPE is donned and removed in a set order. OSHA's Bloodborne Pathogens Standard protects workers from HIV and hepatitis B.",
    ], ["Hand hygiene is most important", "Standard Precautions for everyone", "Airborne, droplet, contact precautions", "Remove gloves first when doffing"]),
    (7, "Safety and Body Mechanics", [
        "Body mechanics use alignment, a wide base of support, and bending at the knees to prevent injury. Common resident injuries include falls, burns, poisoning, and choking. Fall prevention includes answering call lights promptly, keeping beds low, using nonskid footwear, and keeping walkways clear.",
        "Restraints are used only with a doctor's order and limit freedom; restraint alternatives are preferred. Fire safety uses RACE (Remove, Activate, Contain, Extinguish) and PASS for extinguishers. Oxygen safety and reporting hazards are also covered.",
    ], ["Bend at the knees; wide base of support", "Keep beds low and call lights in reach", "Restraints need an order; use alternatives", "RACE and PASS for fire"]),
    (8, "Emergency Care, First Aid, and Disasters", [
        "In emergencies, NAs stay calm, call for help, and provide care within their training. Skills include CPR, the Heimlich maneuver (abdominal thrusts) for choking, and responding to shock, heart attack, stroke, seizures, fainting, bleeding, burns, and diabetic emergencies.",
        "Disaster plans cover fires, floods, tornadoes, and other emergencies. NAs know evacuation routes and their roles.",
    ], ["Stay calm and call for help", "Abdominal thrusts for choking", "Know stroke signs", "Know evacuation routes"]),
    (9, "Admission, Transfer, Discharge, and Physical Exams", [
        "Admission is often stressful for residents; NAs welcome them, orient them to the room, and collect baseline measurements such as height and weight. Inventory of belongings protects personal property.",
        "Transfers and discharges are done carefully and kindly. NAs assist with physical exams by preparing equipment, positioning, and draping residents for privacy.",
    ], ["Admission is stressful; be welcoming", "Measure baseline height and weight", "Inventory belongings", "Drape for privacy during exams"]),
    (10, "Bedmaking and Unit Care", [
        "A resident's unit is their home. NAs keep it clean, safe, and personalized. Beds can be closed, open, occupied, or surgical.",
        "Bedmaking uses clean, wrinkle-free linens with mitered corners; dirty linen is held away from the uniform and never placed on the floor. Occupied beds are made by rolling the resident side to side.",
    ], ["Unit is the resident's home", "Wrinkle-free linens prevent pressure injuries", "Hold dirty linen away from your uniform", "Occupied bed: roll resident side to side"]),
    (11, "Positioning, Moving, and Lifting", [
        "Residents who can't move themselves are repositioned at least every 2 hours to prevent pressure injuries. Positions include Fowler's (sitting), supine, lateral (side), prone, and Sims'.",
        "NAs use gait belts for transfers, lifts for dependent residents, draw sheets to move residents up in bed, and logrolling for spinal injuries. Dangling (sitting on the edge of the bed) before standing prevents dizziness.",
    ], ["Reposition at least every 2 hours", "Fowler's = semi-sitting", "Gait belt for transfers", "Dangle before standing"]),
    (12, "Personal Care", [
        "Personal care includes bathing (complete bed bath, partial bath, tub, shower), perineal care (wash front to back), oral care (including denture care and special care for unconscious residents), shaving, hair and nail care, and dressing.",
        "NAs promote independence and privacy. When dressing a resident with weakness on one side, put clothing on the weak side first and remove it from the strong side first. Diabetic residents' nails are cared for only by a nurse or podiatrist.",
    ], ["Perineal care: front to back", "Dress weak side first; undress strong side first", "Diabetic nail care by nurse or podiatrist", "Promote independence and privacy"]),
    (13, "Vital Signs", [
        "Vital signs are temperature, pulse, respirations, and blood pressure; pain is also measured. Normal ranges: oral temperature about 97.6–99.6°F, pulse 60–100 beats per minute, respirations 12–20 per minute, and blood pressure below 120/80.",
        "Temperature sites include oral, rectal (highest), axillary (lowest), and tympanic. The radial pulse is most common. Count respirations without telling the resident. Blood pressure has systolic (top) and diastolic (bottom) numbers. Report abnormal readings promptly.",
    ], ["Pulse 60–100; respirations 12–20", "Rectal temp highest; axillary lowest", "Count respirations without telling the resident", "Report abnormal vital signs"]),
    (14, "Nutrition and Fluid Balance", [
        "Good nutrition uses all food groups and adequate fluids. Special diets include liquid, soft, mechanical soft, low-sodium, diabetic, and pureed diets. Dysphagia (difficulty swallowing) may require thickened liquids and careful positioning.",
        "NAs assist with feeding, sitting at eye level and offering small bites, and watch for choking and aspiration. Intake and output (I&O) are measured in milliliters, and dehydration and edema are reported. Residents should sit upright during and after meals.",
    ], ["Sit upright for meals", "Thickened liquids for dysphagia", "Measure I&O in mL", "Report signs of dehydration"]),
    (15, "The Gastrointestinal System", [
        "The GI system digests food and eliminates waste. Normal aging slows digestion and can cause constipation. Disorders include GERD, ulcers, constipation, diarrhea, fecal incontinence, and colorectal cancer.",
        "NAs assist with bowel elimination using bedpans and commodes, give enemas only if allowed, collect stool specimens, and care for ostomies (an opening for waste on the abdomen). Report changes in bowel habits or blood in stool.",
    ], ["Aging slows digestion", "Ostomy = surgical opening for waste", "Report blood in stool", "Provide privacy during elimination"]),
    (16, "The Urinary System", [
        "The urinary system removes wastes and maintains fluid balance. Disorders include urinary tract infections (more common in women), urinary incontinence, kidney stones, and chronic kidney disease.",
        "NAs assist with toileting, perineal care, catheter care (keep the bag below the bladder and the tubing free of kinks), urine specimens (clean-catch, 24-hour), and bladder retraining. Report cloudy, dark, or bloody urine and pain.",
    ], ["Keep catheter bag below the bladder", "Clean-catch urine specimens", "UTIs more common in women", "Report cloudy or bloody urine"]),
    (17, "The Reproductive System", [
        "The reproductive system allows reproduction and produces hormones. Normal aging changes include menopause in women and an enlarged prostate in men.",
        "Disorders include benign prostatic hypertrophy, vaginitis, sexually transmitted infections, and reproductive cancers. Residents have the right to sexual expression and privacy.",
    ], ["Menopause = end of menstruation", "Enlarged prostate is common in older men", "Respect residents' sexuality and privacy", "Report discharge or pain"]),
    (18, "The Integumentary System", [
        "The skin protects the body, regulates temperature, and senses touch. Aging skin is thinner, drier, and more fragile.",
        "Pressure injuries develop over bony areas; prevention includes repositioning, keeping skin clean and dry, good nutrition, and special mattresses. NAs report red or open areas immediately. Other conditions include skin tears, fungal infections, and burns.",
    ], ["Aging skin is fragile", "Pressure injuries over bony areas", "Keep skin clean and dry", "Report redness immediately"]),
    (19, "The Circulatory or Cardiovascular System", [
        "The heart pumps blood through arteries, veins, and capillaries. Disorders include hypertension, coronary artery disease, angina, myocardial infarction (heart attack), heart failure, and peripheral vascular disease.",
        "NAs measure vital signs, apply anti-embolic stockings, encourage activity as ordered, follow fluid and sodium restrictions, and report chest pain, shortness of breath, or swelling.",
    ], ["Report chest pain immediately", "Heart failure causes edema", "Anti-embolic stockings improve circulation", "Low-sodium diets for heart conditions"]),
    (20, "The Respiratory System", [
        "The respiratory system brings in oxygen and removes carbon dioxide. Disorders include COPD, asthma, pneumonia, tuberculosis, and lung cancer.",
        "NAs position residents upright to ease breathing, help with coughing and deep breathing exercises, collect sputum specimens, check pulse oximetry, and follow oxygen safety: no smoking or open flames.",
    ], ["Upright positioning eases breathing", "Coughing and deep breathing exercises", "No smoking near oxygen", "Pulse oximetry measures oxygen saturation"]),
    (21, "The Musculoskeletal System", [
        "The musculoskeletal system supports and moves the body. Disorders include arthritis, osteoporosis, fractures (especially hip fractures in older adults), and joint replacements.",
        "NAs assist with ambulation using canes, walkers, and crutches, perform range-of-motion exercises, care for residents in casts or with hip replacements (avoid crossing legs), and prevent falls.",
    ], ["Osteoporosis increases fracture risk", "Hip replacement: don't cross legs", "ROM exercises prevent contractures", "Assist with canes and walkers"]),
    (22, "The Nervous System", [
        "The nervous system controls the body. Disorders include stroke (CVA), Parkinson disease, multiple sclerosis, head and spinal cord injuries, and dementia, including Alzheimer disease.",
        "Stroke may cause one-sided weakness (hemiplegia) and aphasia. NAs approach residents on the unaffected side for awareness, but encourage use of the weaker side as directed. For dementia, NAs keep routines, use simple instructions, and manage behaviors such as wandering, agitation, and sundowning with patience.",
    ], ["Stroke: hemiplegia and aphasia", "Dress the weak side first", "Dementia: routines and simple steps", "Sundowning = late-day confusion"]),
    (23, "The Endocrine System", [
        "Endocrine glands produce hormones. Diabetes mellitus is the most common endocrine disorder: type 1 requires insulin, and type 2 is linked to obesity and inactivity. Thyroid disorders are also common.",
        "NAs follow diabetic diets, report signs of hypoglycemia (shakiness, sweating, confusion) and hyperglycemia (thirst, frequent urination), and provide careful foot care because diabetes reduces circulation and sensation.",
    ], ["Diabetes is the most common endocrine disorder", "Hypoglycemia: shaky, sweaty, confused", "Hyperglycemia: thirst, frequent urination", "Careful foot care"]),
    (24, "The Immune and Lymphatic Systems and Cancer", [
        "The immune system defends against infection; the lymphatic system drains fluid and filters it through lymph nodes. HIV attacks the immune system and can lead to AIDS; it spreads through blood and body fluids, not casual contact.",
        "Cancer is abnormal cell growth that can spread. Treatments include surgery, chemotherapy, and radiation, which cause side effects such as nausea, fatigue, and mouth sores. NAs provide comfort, nutrition support, and emotional support.",
    ], ["HIV spreads through blood and body fluids", "Standard Precautions protect against HIV", "Cancer treatment side effects", "Provide comfort and support"]),
    (25, "Rehabilitation and Restorative Care", [
        "Rehabilitation helps residents regain function after illness or injury; restorative care helps them maintain their abilities. Goals focus on independence and quality of life.",
        "NAs encourage self-care, assist with ambulation and adaptive devices, perform range-of-motion exercises, and support bowel and bladder retraining. Small gains should be praised.",
    ], ["Rehabilitation restores function", "Restorative care maintains ability", "Encourage independence", "Praise small gains"]),
    (26, "Subacute Care", [
        "Subacute care is for residents who need more care than typical long-term care but less than a hospital, often after surgery or acute illness.",
        "NAs may assist with residents who have IVs, feeding tubes, tracheostomies, wounds, or who need pre- and postoperative care. NAs observe and report but don't perform sterile or invasive procedures unless trained and allowed.",
    ], ["Between hospital and long-term care", "Observe IVs and tubes; don't adjust", "Postoperative care", "Report changes promptly"]),
    (27, "End-of-Life Care", [
        "Dying residents and their families need comfort and respect. Kübler-Ross's stages of grief are denial, anger, bargaining, depression, and acceptance. Hospice care focuses on comfort rather than cure.",
        "Signs of approaching death include changes in breathing (Cheyne-Stokes), cool skin, and decreased responsiveness. Hearing is often the last sense lost, so NAs keep speaking kindly. Advance directives and DNR orders must be respected. Postmortem care is done with dignity.",
    ], ["Stages: denial, anger, bargaining, depression, acceptance", "Hospice = comfort care", "Hearing is the last sense lost", "Respect DNR orders"]),
    (28, "Your New Position", [
        "Getting a job includes preparing a résumé, completing applications, and interviewing professionally. NAs must pass a state competency exam and be listed on the state registry.",
        "Keeping a job requires dependability, professionalism, continuing education (in-services), managing stress, and accepting feedback. Career growth options include becoming an LPN or RN.",
    ], ["Pass the state competency exam", "Listed on the state registry", "Continuing education (in-services)", "Manage stress"]),
]

